00:02:34.377 --> 00:02:35.460
HARISH GAGGAR: Absolutely.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.140
Thanks, Mengran.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.220
So before 2016, we had
in-house columnar database.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.920
And our team was
spending a lot of time

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.450
in terms of scalability
and performance tuning--

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.620
scalability in
terms of, they kept

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.380
changing the dashboard
database configurations

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.870
to support our database needs.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.786
And they were also
spending a lot

00:02:56.786 --> 00:03:00.660
of time in hardware maintenance
and also the expensive license

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:01.560
renewal.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.770
And on the performance
tuning side,

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.910
the query's performance
was really slow

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.500
because most of them are
coming from the complex

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.140
join on the columnar database.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.120
And it's delaying, overall,
our ETL processing time.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.960
And analyst team were
waiting for a long time

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.550
to see the up-to-date data.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:22.350
So we ran with a lot of the POC.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:26.700
And finally, we
found that BigQuery

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.150
was the best database for us
to support our business needs.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.230
So let me cover what our
data stack looks like now.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:39.750
So from 2016, Credit Karma data
stack growth from 30 terabytes

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.330
to the 1.8 petabytes.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:45.360
And we collect nine
terabytes of data every day.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.280
And we run many model
predictions, also.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.490
So this is all about volume.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:54.317
But also, we build
many API jobs which

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.400
are able to connect the
various different sources.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.160
For example, we are connecting
Salesforce for finance data,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.050
and we're connecting
Facebook for media,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.830
and Google AdWords
for marketing ads.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.090
And like that, there
are other API jobs, too,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.960
which we run on every day,
and land it with a BigQuery

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.845
and do our Looker
reporting on that.

00:04:13.845 --> 00:04:17.745
On the other side of the other
type of jobs, which is mostly

00:04:17.745 --> 00:04:20.250
the machine learning
models, that,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.750
we're also running
on the BigQuery.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:26.950
So for example, if you want to
forecast our daily active users

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.880
and if you want to forecast
how many emails we want to send

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.710
and what kind of
offer we want to send,

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.820
and if you want to predict
our scoring rates--

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.237
so all these different
models, also, we are running.

00:04:38.237 --> 00:04:40.820
So I would like to thank Google
and their entire support team,

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.530
who really helped us to
brought to this position.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.260
And it does also help us in
our day-to-day business value.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.390
Now, we have three
different use cases,

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.050
which is talk more about
how we leverage a BigQuery

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.330
on our day-to-day life.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.330
So first use case is daily
active user forecast.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.100
I will let Mengran
to try this one.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.225
MENGRAN HE: Thanks, Harish.

00:05:02.225 --> 00:05:05.340
So let me define daily
active user for a second.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.150
So daily active user
is a metric to measure

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:11.410
the number of members who
come to our site every day.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.790
And it can be reflected
by the way we connect

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.635
with our members,
and also measured

00:05:15.635 --> 00:05:18.020
by the product performance.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.700
So a better daily active
user forecast model

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.320
would allow our stakeholders to
monitor the product performance

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.210
in a very prompt way.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.760
And we use Google
BigQuery database

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.390
to store the model inputs
and also the forecast result,

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:35.780
and then migrate the result
from the BigQuery database

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.720
to Looker dashboard so
that our stakeholders can

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.180
monitor the performance
and then compare

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.940
against the actual daily active
user on the dashboard directly.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.930
So in this way, they can
improve the speed and quality

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:52.886
of their business decision for
engaging with our active users.

00:05:52.886 --> 00:05:56.220
Prior to the current,
improved data infrastructure

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.220
and the pipeline,
we had to train

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.010
the model manually every time
we launched a new initiative.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.980
So it cost a long
turn-around time

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.460
for adjusting those impact
of the new initiatives.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.970
And also, we had to collect data
from different channel owners

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.060
or organizations, which
means it's hard for us

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.390
to unify the model input and
then produce the output very

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.730
promptly for our stakeholders.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.860
Because the data
collection is siloed,

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:25.670
it adds up the layer of channel
dependency onto the pipeline.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:29.870
And also, human error might be
involved during the process.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:31.880
So Harish, would
you please tell us

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.760
how business intelligence
team at Credit Karma

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:38.700
solved those challenges and
improved the infrastructure?

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.140
HARISH GAGGAR: Absolutely.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.530
We have an automated
data pipeline system

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:46.320
which connects our
expected email click

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.210
ratios and the actual
email click ratios.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.045
And then we forecast
how actual users

00:06:51.045 --> 00:06:54.240
are going to perform on
the email what we send.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.150
On this data pipeline,
we use Airflow,

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.500
which is an open-source
tool from Airbnb.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.680
This is for managing
the complex workflows

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.390
and scheduling the jobs.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.270
First thing is, I would
like to cover how we

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.460
drive the expected email click.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.880
So in this, our
pipeline is first

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.770
reading the Google spreadsheet.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.230
The Google spreadsheet, we
are running through Python.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:23.670
And the Excel sheet
is mostly maintained

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:26.640
by our campaign managers,
where, day-to-day basis,

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.460
they are continuously updating
our expected email and push

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.320
notification click volume.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.950
Once the Python program
learns [INAUDIBLE],,

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.200
it loads the data
back to the BigQuery.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.510
And then on the other side, we
connect back the BigQuery again

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.390
for our actual data.

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:44.970
So once we have
our expected email

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.280
and push notification
data and the actual email

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.820
and push notification data,
we combine both this data

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.360
and prepare our data
set for the model.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.710
Then, we send this data back
to our machine learning model.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.750
And that machine
learning model trying

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:04.785
to predict the daily users--

00:08:04.785 --> 00:08:06.244
how they're going
to take an email,

00:08:06.244 --> 00:08:07.868
how they're going to
read those emails,

00:08:07.868 --> 00:08:09.960
how they're going to
perform over those emails.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.890
And then we send this forecast
number back to the BigQuery.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:17.430
And ultimately, then, we connect
this Looker to the BigQuery

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.956
to see the overall performance
on our daily forecast.

00:08:20.956 --> 00:08:22.080
MENGRAN HE: Thanks, Harish.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.280
So because of those improvements
on the data pipeline,

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.740
we got the chance to improve the
flexibility and the robustness

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:32.789
of our forecast model, just like
the daily active user forecast.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:35.700
And also, our stakeholders can
get a better and up-to-date

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.039
report directly on
the Looker dashboard

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:39.750
for their business planning.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:41.850
And they also can
monitor the product

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:46.130
and the channel performance on
the Looker dashboard directly.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:50.380
And it improved the
working efficiency a lot.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:54.160
And next, Harish will talk
about how Credit Karma used

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.030
machine learning tools to
improve the email targeting

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.579
efficiency.

00:08:59.579 --> 00:09:00.370
HARISH GAGGAR: Yes.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:01.036
Thanks, Mengran.

00:09:01.036 --> 00:09:04.250
So here, in this
particular use case,

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.710
we are trying to improve the
overall targeting efficiency

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.480
on the email notification.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.180
Before I proceed on
the remaining slide,

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:18.670
let me talk more about
what model-driven email is.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.910
Model-driven email is a
machine learning model

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:23.800
where, especially,
we are looking

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.050
for what kind of offer
users are searching--

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.630
for example, if they are
looking for a credit card

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:30.670
or personal loan
or a mortgage loan.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:36.360
So based on the requirement,
we predict the users

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:38.620
who most likely tend
to get those e-mails,

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.230
and who also like
to open those emails

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:44.830
and click on those emails, and
then take the offer from us.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.770
I will cover the data
pipeline more in detail.

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:49.570
But I want to first
explain what are

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.950
the challenges we had before.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:54.890
So these are the
couple of points here.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:10:00.120
We have a huge amount
of variables to support.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.990
So we have 80
million-plus members.

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.750
And there are 1,000-plus
input variables.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.007
It was very difficult
and it was very

00:10:08.007 --> 00:10:09.590
time-consuming and
resource-intensive,

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:12.350
too, to collect all
this data collection,

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.600
and then send those data back
to the model, and then score,

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.050
and then send to the users.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.510
It was almost a
nightmare for us to run

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:23.550
those huge amount of data
on the columnar database.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:28.770
And there was no manual check--

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:31.460
I mean, there were no quality
checks, also, in place.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.450
And we don't know
what are the data

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.980
anomaly we found over
those emails and variables.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:40.190
Many variables are coming
with a blind values,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.660
or they don't have any values.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.710
So there was no point in
running a model on such values.

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:47.900
So it was very difficult
to identify such problems

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:51.905
without an automated
[INAUDIBLE] system.

00:10:51.905 --> 00:10:56.570
I will talk more about how
we solved those challenges.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:00.230
Again, we use
Airflow to implement

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:01.730
such end-to-end pipeline.

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:07.040
This pipeline helps
us to collect the data

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.010
over all the users who
perform on the emails

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:11.450
in the last few months.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.060
And we collect all those
data, run the scoring,

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:16.100
and then, finally,
send out an email.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.820
So let me start with a
little bit more detail

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.900
on how this pipeline works.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.330
This pipeline actually
works on the three models.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.240
One is the impression to click.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.830
That means that when we send
out an email to our users

00:11:29.830 --> 00:11:32.540
and they saw the email and
they click on that email,

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:34.130
that means impression to click.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:37.030
And the second model is
click to applications--

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:40.910
when they try to click
on the offers and they

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:42.350
try to fill all
their information

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:45.300
on the partner's site and
then submit an application.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.240
And the third
model is approval--

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.490
once they submit an
application, their application's

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:50.930
sent to our partners.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:54.200
And then our partners
make a decision

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:57.530
whether they get approval
on their offers or not.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:01.760
So in the first step,
in the Google BigQuery,

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.480
we try to collect all
this three model data.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:09.020
So for impression to click
and click to application,

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:12.470
we collect those data
points from the user recent

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:13.820
[INAUDIBLE] history--

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:17.090
how they perform over the
email in the past few months.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:18.860
And for the approval
model, we rely

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:21.040
on our online
recommendation system team,

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:24.110
where they given us more idea
about what are the approval

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:25.790
odds for our members.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:30.320
So once we collect all
this three model data set,

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.630
we run the queries, and
then we store the data

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.300
into the Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.060
That is for preparing
the training data set.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.340
Then, once the
data set is ready,

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:42.690
we send those data
back to the TensorFlow.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:46.790
So TensorFlow is helping us on--

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:50.804
this is another cloud service
we get from Google where

00:12:50.804 --> 00:12:52.970
we are able to run our
machine learning model on all

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:55.500
this three data set.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.780
So this model is actually
scoring every member

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:02.150
on the thousands of
variables, just to make sure

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:06.470
that we make a right
ranking on every member,

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:08.850
that what offers they're
supposed to receive.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:12.410
So the scoring is calculated
entirely through this model.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:16.130
And again, store it
back to the BigQuery.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.340
So once we have our
scoring set ready,

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.110
now we want to
drive the scoring,

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:24.011
and now we want to drive
our scoring data set

00:13:24.011 --> 00:13:25.010
through the Excel sheet.

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:28.190
So this Excel sheet, it's
all about the campaign sheet.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.450
Here, we maintain the
different email campaigns.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:31.880
So our campaign
managers come in,

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:34.190
and they keep adding
the new campaigns,

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:36.101
and they're modifying
the existing campaigns.

00:13:36.101 --> 00:13:38.100
And they change the
behavior of those campaigns.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.400
That means, for
example, if I want

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.890
to send out an email
to the users who

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:48.710
have a score between 600 or 700
with an age range of 30 to 35--

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:53.070
so they change those criteria,
and they look for what offer

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:54.860
we need to send to those users.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.860
So they select all those
criteria over the Excel sheet.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:01.220
Our pipeline would automatically
read this Excel sheet

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:03.800
and combine this scoring
data set together.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:06.770
And then we run our targeting
queries in the BigQuery.

00:14:06.770 --> 00:14:08.210
So those targeting
queries are all

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:11.770
about figure it out, who
are eligible for sending out

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:12.800
a new email.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.470
So once we have those
targeting queries ready.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:17.960
Then we send this data
back to the Hermes.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:21.070
Hermes is an internal
tool which allows

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:25.040
us to send out an
email to our members

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:26.904
with their specific offer needs.

00:14:26.904 --> 00:14:28.320
MENGRAN HE: And,
Harish, would you

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:31.230
please introduce what
Hermes is at Credit Karma

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:34.660
and how we utilize Hermes to
send the email to our targeting

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:35.430
users?

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:36.680
HARISH GAGGAR: Great question.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:41.190
So Hermes is an internal tool
for the user-level targeting

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:46.530
where we can allow marketing
analysts to write their own SQL

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:50.370
query and decide the
range of the users

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:53.590
to whom they want to send an
email and what kind of offer

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:55.045
they want to filter out.

00:14:55.045 --> 00:14:56.170
MENGRAN HE: Thanks, Harish.

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:01.460
HARISH GAGGAR: Overall,
with this data pipeline,

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:02.210
it's helped a lot.

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:07.370
It's not just improved overall
efficiencies-- also improve--

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:11.810
it help us to send out the
right offer to the users.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:15.950
This helps us in, over all,
our engagement on the emails.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:19.730
And also, it reduced
the overall resources

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:24.250
to collect the data every
time and refresh the data,

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.290
run the model, and send out
up-to-date emails every time.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.100
So this whole
automated system has

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:31.750
reduced a lot of the main
power and also improved

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:34.640
the overall data processing
time and also add a lot of value

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:36.790
to our business.

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:41.440
And we also have Looker pointing
to the email campaigns data

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.050
where we do the
measurement check--

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.450
how each campaign is performing.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:49.020
For example, if you
send a Chase card

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:53.310
to the particular members,
how many members, basically,

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:54.149
open those emails?

00:15:54.149 --> 00:15:55.440
How many of them click on that?

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.390
How many of them take
the offer from us?

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:02.070
So all this, we continuously
drive on the local dashboards.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:06.390
And based on that, we're
going back and re-engineering

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.385
our entire data--

00:16:08.385 --> 00:16:10.260
re-engineering our
model to make sure

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.270
that we have the
right data set and we

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:15.390
have right feature
set to ensure that we

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:19.062
keep delivering the best email
to our members as we can.

00:16:19.062 --> 00:16:20.520
MENGRAN HE: This
is really helpful,

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:23.970
because email is one of our
most efficient way of targeting

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.510
our users, and also how
we engage with our users.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:30.270
Sending a more relevant
message with the right amount

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.420
of frequency are pretty
critical to our business.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:37.030
And next, Harish is going to
wrap up our last use case--

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:39.750
how we obtained a better
model for user acquisition

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:44.090
and a better offer pricing
on the search media.

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:45.220
HARISH GAGGAR: Absolutely.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.290
So this is one of our
end of use case, where

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:51.250
we are trying to improve
the overall user engagement

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.160
and user experience
when they are

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:57.610
looking for any offer on the
page search and display media.

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:04.960
So how this work
is, we have a Google

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.720
written on [INAUDIBLE]
algorithm that

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:12.550
allow us to bid in auctions
at the user level granularity.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:15.040
And this also help
us in overall quality

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:19.359
of the new user engagement
based on their monetizations.

00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:21.250
So monetization's
all about how users

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:24.880
click on emails in the
past and how they took

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:28.069
the offers over the GoogleAds.

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:32.000
And ultimately, this
help us to send a--

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:36.800
it increases overall efficiency
in sending the overall revenue

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:40.460
to the [? CK ?] and
based on our target.

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:46.290
So there are, like before,
I explained more about how

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:50.790
this data pipeline works, how
we improve the automated ad

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:53.370
campaigns, the features.

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.882
Let me first explain what are
the challenges we had before,

00:17:56.882 --> 00:18:01.775
why we got less traffic
in the past over our ads,

00:18:01.775 --> 00:18:05.710
and how we get the flat
value on every transaction.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.250
So we had this flat
to flat rate concept

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:11.880
initially on every credit
card application of our ads.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:19.040
This flat to flat value also
means that what kind of users

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:21.930
come to Google and take the
offer from us in the past.

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:27.660
And This base, also,
is all about these user

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.820
sets are coming from
different user segments.

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.520
Like, for example, they are
coming with a different score

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.820
band and age band.

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:40.350
They are coming with a specific
platform, like mobile or web.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:43.830
And then they are coming in
the last seven days or 10 days,

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:45.580
or any months, or not.

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:49.620
And it's also about
they are coming

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.370
from the organic or
display media or not.

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:53.700
So based on these
different media searches,

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:56.940
and based on the
overall mixed traffic,

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:01.980
we were defining the average
weight on the offers.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:08.575
So this average rate scoring,
it was good for the campaign

00:19:08.575 --> 00:19:12.690
analytics, but it was not good
for the user-level scoring.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:18.200
So ultimately, we were getting
the flat rate for every credit

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:19.590
card transitions.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:23.020
That actually slowed down
the overall revenue growth.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:27.000
And we also had a difficulty
in identifying the issues

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.290
in the pipeline.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:31.420
For example, when I talk
about, in the earlier point,

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.860
how the different user
segments come in on the site,

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:36.990
and they take the
offer from us--

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:42.150
there was the issue where data
is not normally distributed.

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:45.720
And we were not sure that what
kind of volumes are coming

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.710
and how the date is coming.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:50.790
And ultimately, we are
spending a lot of money

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:53.370
on our ad campaigns.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:20:02.580
So overall, it was impacting
our transition to revenue

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:06.720
on our credit card
applications on the ads.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.090
So this is how we
solved the challenges.

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:13.440
We built the data pipeline,
which automatically collects

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:18.160
all the user history, how they
perform over the GoogleAds.

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:25.950
So this is like, for
example, if users are

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:28.360
coming with a specific segment.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:31.380
So we make sure that we
collect all the data set

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:32.890
for a few months.

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:38.200
So what we did is, we've entered
in our database in a BigQuery.

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:39.630
And then we figure it out--

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:41.230
what the user behaviors are.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:43.320
For example, it's not
all about five segments.

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:45.450
It's also about
thousands of variables

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:46.710
for that given member--

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.320
how he actually came to a
site, at which time he came in,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:51.420
from where he came
in, and what kind of

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:53.641
offer he were looking for--

00:20:53.641 --> 00:20:55.620
not just on our website.

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:57.900
It's also overall on the media.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:00.310
And we collect all
these data points.

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:03.780
And then we store them in
the Google Cloud Storage.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.690
And then we run our
machine learning model

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:12.390
to figure out the propensity
of buying the offer from us.

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:13.980
So once we have
this data set ready,

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.180
we send it back to the Google.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:20.410
The Google AdWords has their
own algorithm [INAUDIBLE],,

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.700
which is written on
[? Adspin. ?] That algorithm

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:24.990
collects this data set.

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:29.340
And that's run their own
scoring on those data set.

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:31.140
And then it's determined
that, hey, this

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:35.280
is the users most
tends to likely take

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:37.260
this particular
offer from our ads.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:40.800
And we ultimately send all
this data back to the BigQuery.

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:43.350
And then we have a
Looker again, which

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.720
is connecting to the
BigQuery, and see

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:51.300
the overall performance-- how
these things are happening,

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:54.210
what kind of users are coming
every day, what kind of offers

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.720
they are looking for, and
where we can improve more.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:03.590
So ultimately, this
helps us a lot.

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:06.350
There was no manual work.

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:10.370
Our BigQuery's automatically
executing on a scheduled basis.

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:13.910
They collect all the data
points on the user behavior.

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:16.250
And it automatically is
sent to the Google AdWords.

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:19.070
Google AdWords decide their
own scoring and target.

00:22:19.070 --> 00:22:23.870
So it's a good align with our
incentive between the BigQuery

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:25.550
and the Credit Karma data.

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:31.640
And I would say that it
also helps us in a way

00:22:31.640 --> 00:22:33.620
that it's use our
proprietary data,

00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:38.502
and it makes our media
channel more profitable.

00:22:38.502 --> 00:22:40.710
MENGRAN HE: Thanks, Harish,
for amazing presentation.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:42.630
So from an analyst's
perspective,

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:44.970
it's really critical
and also helpful

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.400
for us to get a
better efficiency

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:50.610
by using Google Cloud
Platform and BigQuery as well

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:53.850
as Looker dashboard, just
like the three examples.

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:55.980
We can definitely reduce
the turnaround time

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:59.670
for generating a forecast
model for our stakeholders.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:03.510
And we can get a higher and
improve the email targeting

00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:04.470
efficiency.

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:07.320
And we also get a better
click-to-conversion ratio

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:09.980
in a very cost-effective way.

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:12.860
And I hope you enjoyed
this webinar session

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:14.310
as much as we did.

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:17.570
And stay tuned for the live
Q&amp;A. And we will be back

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:18.880
in less than a minute.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:51.750
Welcome back.

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:54.570
So let's get started
with some live questions.

00:27:54.570 --> 00:27:55.580
So the first one--

00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:59.120
"Have you used cloud dataflow
for the Pipeline as well?

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:02.810
Also, are you doing this on
a real-time basis or batch

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:04.241
process?"

00:28:04.241 --> 00:28:05.490
HARISH GAGGAR: Great question.

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:08.090
We have not used--

00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:10.510
we have done the
POCs on the dataflow.

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.670
But, yeah, this is nothing
we have put into production.

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:16.940
Mostly, we are using the
dataproc in TensorFlow

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:19.460
for our pipeline.

00:28:19.460 --> 00:28:23.360
And we are also doing
lot of in-house testing

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:25.700
on the real-time model building.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:28.916
But currently, most of them
are on the batch processing.

00:28:28.916 --> 00:28:30.290
MENGRAN HE: "Do
you have anything

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:32.457
to optimize the
performance of BigQuery?"

00:28:32.457 --> 00:28:33.290
HARISH GAGGAR: Yeah.

00:28:33.290 --> 00:28:36.380
It depends what kind of
query you're running for.

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:39.630
If you are using the
Legacy SQL query,

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:42.740
we definitely encourage you to
use the [INAUDIBLE] SQL query

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:45.890
and make sure that you keep
all your joins in mind.

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:49.040
And also, it depends
on at what time

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:50.795
you are running those queries.

00:28:50.795 --> 00:28:54.770
If you are having
a limited license

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:58.780
and you have many analytics
teams using the BigQuery

00:28:58.780 --> 00:29:00.770
at the same time,
I would recommend

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:05.807
that you use those heavy queries
in the batch job in the night.

00:29:05.807 --> 00:29:06.890
MENGRAN HE: Next question.

00:29:06.890 --> 00:29:09.100
"How does Looker
connect to BigQuery?"

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:15.590
HARISH GAGGAR: So in Looker, we
can first define the connection

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:16.310
to the BigQuery.

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.560
On their admin, we
have full control

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.600
of connecting to
[INAUDIBLE] database.

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:23.180
And then, we have
LookML feature,

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:25.715
where you just need to
import the BigQuery table.

00:29:25.715 --> 00:29:27.090
And it automatically
[? builds ?]

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:28.680
the LookML for you.

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:32.030
All you need to do is
just simply refresh

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:36.420
that model and simply drag
and drop and start visualizing

00:29:36.420 --> 00:29:39.050
your data on the BigQuery live.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:40.400
MENGRAN HE: Next question.

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:42.500
"What advice would you
give to someone who

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:45.920
is starting this process now?"

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:49.140
HARISH GAGGAR: That comes
with a lot of things.

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:51.530
For example, it depends on
what kind of infrastructure

00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:56.721
you have, what kind of
instances you want to run.

00:29:56.721 --> 00:30:01.550
If you want to run on a
smaller data set, it's fine.

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:03.620
You can spin up the
small GCP instance,

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:05.460
and then you can create
your small project,

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:08.000
and then you can start
with a limited license,

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:10.290
and you can continue
to go from there.

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.590
But again, if it is coming
through the mid- and

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:16.310
large-scale business,
you have to decide again

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:19.250
on the architecture side
how many instances you need,

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:20.632
how many projects you need.

00:30:20.632 --> 00:30:23.090
Because you ultimately don't
want to one project to create,

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.880
and everyone wants your company
to use the same project because

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:28.340
of limited concurrent
query limit.

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:33.500
So you have to split those
projects out and decide

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.459
your organization numbers--

00:30:35.459 --> 00:30:37.500
how many people who are
going to run the queries,

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:40.340
and what kind of queries
they want to run.

00:30:40.340 --> 00:30:42.300
If you organize it
very well, then yeah,

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:48.470
of course, this is a perfect use
case where you can continuously

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:51.560
use and continuously scale
your instance over the cloud

00:30:51.560 --> 00:30:53.660
and keep running your queries.

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:55.790
MENGRAN HE: And the next
one-- so, "How up-to-date

00:30:55.790 --> 00:30:59.040
is the data that can be
shown on a Looker dashboard?"

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:01.940
HARISH GAGGAR: So that
is also depend on how

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:03.380
you want to refresh the data.

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:05.010
If you want to
refresh on a schedule,

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:08.080
basically, in the
Looker, you can always--

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:12.450
there is an option where you can
create your data SQL queries.

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:16.310
And you can set a trigger
alert for automatically clear

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:17.090
the cache.

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:19.430
So in that way, if someone
go to the Looker dashboard,

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:22.120
it will hit to that
LookerML behind the scenes.

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:25.070
It runs that query and
automatically clears

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:27.640
the cache for you and
show you up-to-date data.

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:30.290
But sometimes, people
don't want to create

00:31:30.290 --> 00:31:31.790
[INAUDIBLE] SQL
queries because this

00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:33.890
comes with a lot of
performance impact, too.

00:31:33.890 --> 00:31:37.820
So they can always go to
their Looker dashboard.

00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:41.120
There is option there, and
you can go ahead and click

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:42.740
on that Clear Cache button.

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:46.430
And that will just simply go
and hit to the BigQuery table

00:31:46.430 --> 00:31:49.373
and give you up-to-date
data on your dashboard.

00:31:49.373 --> 00:31:50.280
MENGRAN HE: Cool.

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:52.010
So next question--
"What made you

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:55.109
decide to switch to BigQuery?"

00:31:55.109 --> 00:31:57.650
HARISH GAGGAR: This is what we
something discussed initially,

00:31:57.650 --> 00:32:02.370
that it depends from
where you are coming from.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:04.810
We came from the
columnar database system.

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:07.430
They come with a lot of
the maintenance issues

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:10.460
and also the performance issues.

00:32:10.460 --> 00:32:12.380
Because our ETL
queries are very heavy,

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:14.820
and they run terabytes
of data every day,

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:17.570
and it's really slowed down
the performance because

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:18.890
of the complex join.

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:21.180
And ultimately, our
ETL was delaying,

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:23.360
and the analytics team's
not getting the data

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:25.990
on the right time.

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:30.320
And also, it was a lot of
maintenance, also, and also

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:33.350
cost of the infrastructure in
terms of licensing renewal.

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:36.680
So if you consider
all these factors,

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:40.280
Google BigQuery is the place
where you can go to the cloud,

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:41.960
you can spin the
instance so quickly.

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:43.200
It's so easy to go there.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.862
There are a lot of
features rightly available.

00:32:45.862 --> 00:32:47.320
You just need to
spin the instance.

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:48.800
You create your project.

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:52.700
And you can always scale
that on demand, basically.

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:55.760
You don't need much interaction
with any support team

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.550
or so, because it's
so self-explanatory.

00:32:58.550 --> 00:33:02.450
You can simply go ahead
and go to that instance,

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:05.450
and decide your own concurrent
limit, define your own cluster,

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:07.802
and start using the database.

00:33:07.802 --> 00:33:08.510
MENGRAN HE: Cool.

